# What do we know so far, and what don't we?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Which big questions does the rest of the course chase? Test: scarcity, trade, prices and measuring wealth"
next: {"title":"Do markets work — and what would that even mean?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/13-do-markets-work-and-what-would-that-even.cast"}

## Four tools
sources:
    - id: alston1992
      title: Is There a Consensus Among Economists in the 1990's?
      authors: Alston, R. M., Kearl, J. R. & Vaughan, M. B.
      year: 1992
      finding: 'In a 1990 survey of economists employed in the United States, more than 90% agreed (with or without provisos) that tariffs and import quotas usually reduce general economic welfare. American Economic Review 82(2): 203–209.'

    box t_scar "Scarcity\nopportunity\ncost" width 230 height 320 radius 10 icon hourglass font_size 30 in map hidden true
    box t_trade "Trade\ncomparative\nadvantage" width 230 height 320 radius 10 icon handshake font_size 30 in map hidden true
    box t_price "Prices\nsupply &\ndemand" width 230 height 320 radius 10 icon "balance scale" font_size 30 in map hidden true
    box t_wealth "Wealth\nGDP per person\nat PPP" width 230 height 320 radius 10 icon "bar chart" font_size 30 in map hidden true
    row map gap 30 fit.h 390 fit.w 960 fit.x 20 fit.y 250 hidden true
    text survey "Tariffs usually hurt:\n9 in 10 economists agree (1990)" #2f6b8f at.percent [50,12] cites ["alston1992"] font_size 34 hidden true
    cards claims hidden true
        Mostly agreed: Free tickets cost, Trade can help both
        * "Less supply, dearer" bin "Mostly agreed"
        Still argued: Why nations got rich, GDP = wellbeing?, Who gains from trade
    text sure_logic "The logic: consensus" #2f6b8f at.percent [50,17] font_size 34 hidden true
    text sure_gaps "What it leaves out: contested / open" #b5482e at.percent [50,6] font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "What we know so far"

What do we actually know about the economy so far, and what don' (@draw t_scar@)t we? Here are the four tools on one page.

B: The first is scarcity. Every choice costs the best thing you gave up for it: its opportunity cost.
    highlight t_scar

Second, trade. Two people both gain when each does what costs the (@highlight t_trade@)m least to give up, even if one of them is better at everything.
    draw t_trade

B: Third, prices. Nobody sets them; they settle where supply meets d (@highlight t_price@)emand, and they follow the last unit bought, not the total value.
    draw t_price

And fourth, wealth. We count it as GDP per person, compared at l (@highlight t_wealth@)ocal prices so a haircut in Delhi counts like a haircut in Oslo.
    draw t_wealth

B: Fine tools. But does anyone actually agree on what they say?
    highlight map effect box

On some things, remarkably so. In a 1990 survey,  (@draw survey@)more than nine in ten economists working in America agreed, at least with provisos, that tariffs and import quotas usually make a country worse off.

B: And on other things they have argued for two centuries. Try sorting a few claims yourself.
    erase survey

    fade map to 0

    draw claims

    ask "Agreed, or still argued? Sort each card." on claims stage own store srt voice b
        right: {srt} of {srt.total}: the tools' logic is agreed; the big whys are argued.
        wrong: {srt} of {srt.total}: the tools' logic is agreed; the big whys are argued.

    erase claims

    fade map to 1

The tool (@draw sure_logic@)s' logic is close to consensus. What they leave out, who (@draw sure_gaps@) gains, why some countries got rich, what GDP misses, is contested, and some of it is still open.

B: Which is no reason to drop them. So let's see whether they work on cases we haven't met.
    highlight map

## A new loaf
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Loaves (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand: {}
    supply: {}
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    supply_shift:
      amount: 0
      label: S′
    demand_shift:
      amount: 0
      label: D′

    card title "Test: a new loaf"

    hide supply_shift_arrow demand_shift_arrow

B: First test, on prices. A new case: the bread a (@draw equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar@)t Mia's bakery in Harbourtown.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S

B: Say the price of flour jumps, so every loaf costs Mia more to bake. Which curve moves, and which way?
    highlight supply_curve demand_curve

    ask "Where does Mia's supply go? Move it, then press Done." on supply_curve predict true store t voice b
        right: Yes. {t.why}
        wrong: Not quite. {t.why}

B: Supply shif (@animate supply_shift.amount -25 duration 3@)ts left: at every price, Mia now offers fewer loaves. Bread gets dearer, and less of it is sold.
    draw supply_shift_curve label_S_shift

    fade supply_curve label_S to 0.3

So her customers just shrug and pay the new price?
    dot cross #3d3833 at.intersection_of ["demand_shift_curve","supply_shift_curve"]

B: Some do; some buy less. They slide up their demand curve, but what they want at each price hasn't changed.
    highlight demand_curve

Then, the same month, a cheap rival bakery o (@animate demand_shift.amount -12 duration 3@)pens on the quay, and fewer people want Mia's bread at any price.
    draw demand_shift_curve label_D_shift

    fade demand_curve label_D to 0.3

    quiz "Flour got dearer and a cheap rival opened, both in the same month. What can we say for sure about Mia's bread?"
        right: Fewer loaves: both shifts cut the quantity, while they push the price in opposite directions.
        wrong: Do the two changes push the price the same way? Do they push the quantity the same way?
        + Fewer loaves sold
        * A higher price
        * A lower price

B: Both changes cut the loaves Mia sells. On the price, they pull in opposite directions, so it depends on which shift is bigger.
    highlight cross label_Qstar

B: Pause and drag the curves: find a mix where Mia's price ends up below where it started.
    explore

So the tool gives a sure answer on one side and a 'depends' on the other, and it tells us exactly what the 'depends' depends on.
    highlight cross effect circle

## Inside the frontier
domain: {"x":[0,520],"y":[0,140]}
sources:
    - id: lebergott1957
      title: Annual Estimates of Unemployment in the United States, 1900–1954
      authors: Lebergott, S.
      year: 1957
      finding: 'US unemployment in 1933: 24.9% of the civilian labour force (12.8 million), the standard estimate, counting people on work relief as unemployed (Darby''s series, counting them as employed, is lower). NBER, The Measurement and Behavior of Unemployment.'
      url: https://www.nber.org/chapters/c2644.pdf

    axes ax x_label "Bread (loaves)" y_label "Fish (crates)" ticks.x [200,400] ticks.y [50,100] hidden true
    curve frontier #b5482e expr 100*sqrt(1-(x/400)^2) x_from 0 x_to 400 hidden true
    label lab_frontier "frontier" right attach_to frontier hidden true
    dot pA at.data [110,45] radius 9 hidden true
    label lab_A "A" below attach_to pA hidden true
    dot pB at.data [240,80] radius 9 hidden true
    label lab_B "B" above-right attach_to pB hidden true
    dot pC at.data [340,95] radius 9 hidden true
    label lab_C "C" above attach_to pC hidden true
    label lab_unreach "out of reach" right attach_to pC hidden true
    dot idle #8f887c at.data [130,35] radius 10 style.fill #8f887c hidden true
    label lab_idle "half the boatyard idle" below-right attach_to idle hidden true
    text stat "1933: about 1 US worker in 4 jobless" #2f6b8f at.data [230,128] cites ["lebergott1957"] font_size 28 hidden true
    portrait keynes x 860 y 470 width 170 of "John Maynard Keynes" cameo true hidden true
    arrow q_unemp #2f6b8f from.data [140,42] to.data [205,82] hidden true
    label lab_q_unemp "Why unemployment?" left #2f6b8f attach_to q_unemp hidden true
    arrow q_mkt #8a5fa8 from.data [150,95] to.data [310,66] hidden true
    label lab_q_mkt "Do markets work?" below #8a5fa8 attach_to q_mkt hidden true
    curve outer dashed #d0865f expr 125*sqrt(1-(x/500)^2) x_from 0 x_to 500 hidden true
    arrow q_grow #d0865f from.data [370,40] to.data [455,52] hidden true
    text lab_q_grow "How do we grow?" #d0865f at.data [488,88] font_size 26 hidden true
    text sure "All three: contested" #b5482e at.data [430,122] font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Inside the frontier"

The price tool gave a 'depends'. Scarcity gives a sharper test: Ha (@draw frontier lab_frontier@)rbourtown's frontier of fish and bread, with illustrative numbers.
    draw ax

B: Under the curve is everything the town can make this year; on it, ever (@draw pA lab_A pB lab_B pC lab_C parallel true@)yone is working flat out. Which of these three points can't it reach?

    ask "Which point can't the town reach? Tap it." choose ["pA","pB","pC"] answer pC voice b
        right: C: it lies beyond the frontier, more fish and more bread than the town's people can make.
        wrong: Which point lies outside the curve of the most the town can make?

Beyond the frontier is the one place scarcity forbids: no plan and no price gets the town there this year.
    draw lab_unreach

B: Now my objection. Everything we've built assumes the town sits on its frontier. What if the boatyard lays off half its workers?
    erase pA lab_A pC lab_C lab_unreach lab_B

Then the town sits deep inside: less fish and less bread, and nobody gains from the waste.
    move pB to.data [130,35] ghost true duration 2

    erase pB

    draw idle lab_idle

B: And scarcity alone says that shouldn't last: idle hands and unmet wants should find each other. Yet it does last.
    highlight idle

In 1933, on the st (@draw stat@)andard estimate, about one American worker in four had no job. John Maynard Ke (@draw keynes@)ynes wrote his General Theory to explain how an economy could stay stuck there.

    erase keynes stat

B: So one frontier gives us three questions, not (@draw q_unemp lab_q_unemp@) one. First: why do economies sit inside it?

Second: do markets get us onto it, and to a good point on it?
    draw q_mkt lab_q_mkt

B: And third: what pushes the whole frontier out, decade after decade?
    draw outer q_grow lab_q_grow

How sure are we on any of the three? Conte (@draw sure@)sted, all of them. That's why they fill the rest of the course.

    quiz "A new harbour crane lets the same crews land twice the fish. What happens on the picture?"
        intro: Three last questions, on new cases.
        right: The frontier moves out, mostly up the fish axis: the same people can now make more than before.
        wrong: With the crane, can the same people make more than before?
        + The frontier moves out
        * The town slides along the frontier
        * The town moves inside the frontier

    quiz "Ola can make 4 nets or 2 oars in a day; Sara 1 net or 1 oar. Who should make the oars?" voice b
        right: Sara: an oar costs her one net, but costs Ola two. Ola is better at both and still gains by making nets.
        wrong: Count what each gives up, in nets, to make one oar.
        * Ola
        + Sara
        * Nobody gains from splitting the work

    fade ax frontier lab_frontier idle lab_idle q_unemp lab_q_unemp q_mkt lab_q_mkt outer q_grow lab_q_grow sure to 0

    scale ppp label "income per person at PPP" unit $ min 0 max 10000 value 8000

    ask "A country earns $2,000 per person at market exchange rates, and its prices are about a quarter of US prices. Click its income per person at PPP." on ppp store g relative true default "2000"
        right: About {g.true}: you said {g}. Each dollar buys four times as much there.
        wrong: You said {g}; it is about {g.true}. If everything costs a quarter as much, each dollar buys four times as much.

    erase ppp

    fade ax frontier lab_frontier idle lab_idle q_unemp lab_q_unemp q_mkt lab_q_mkt outer q_grow lab_q_grow sure to 1

So what we know is how to use four tools; what we don't yet know is when an economy fails to use them all, and the first question we chase is whether markets work at all.
    highlight frontier idle

